# PPS clients configuration
#

comment "PPS clients support"

config PPS_CLIENT_KTIMER
	tristate "Kernel timer client (Testing client, use for debug)"
	help
	  If you say yes here you get support for a PPS debugging client
	  which uses a kernel timer to generate the PPS signal.

	  This driver can also be built as a module.  If so, the module
	  will be called pps-ktimer.

config PPS_CLIENT_LDISC
	tristate "PPS line discipline"
	depends on TTY
	help
	  If you say yes here you get support for a PPS source connected
	  with the CD (Carrier Detect) pin of your serial port.

config PPS_CLIENT_PARPORT
	tristate "Parallel port PPS client"
	depends on PARPORT
	help
	  If you say yes here you get support for a PPS source connected
	  with the interrupt pin of your parallel port.

config PPS_CLIENT_GPIO
	tristate "PPS client using GPIO"
	help
	  If you say yes here you get support for a PPS source using
	  GPIO. To be useful you must also register a platform device
	  specifying the GPIO pin and other options, usually in your board
	  setup.

config PPS_CLIENT_IRQ
	tristate "PPS client using an interrupt"
	depends on OF
	help
	  Capture PPS events from an interrupt controller. This supports
	  signals routed directly to an interrupt without a GPIO consumer.
	  Select a single rising or falling edge in the device tree.
	  Configure the expected assert period and allowed phase jitter.
	  The driver is called pps-irq when built as a module.

config PPS_CLIENT_IRQ_KUNIT_TEST
	tristate "PPS interrupt phase qualification tests" if !KUNIT_ALL_TESTS
	depends on KUNIT
	default KUNIT_ALL_TESTS
	help
	  Test phase acquisition, comparator wraparound pulses, missing
	  pulses, jitter limits and recovery after an output interruption
	  or a phase change with continuous rejected edges.
	  The tests run without PPS hardware or an interrupt controller.
	  This suite is called pps-irq-phase.
